["No matter what with 不管…都…","不管 + A（或 B），都 + result","不管 (bùguǎn) means “no matter”. After it you give a question word, or two choices joined by 还是 (háishi). 都 (dōu) in the next part says that the result is always the same. 无论 (wúlùn) has the same meaning and is more formal.",[["不管","bùguǎn","no matter"],["下雨","xià yǔ","to rain"],["还是","háishi","or"],["下雪","xià xuě","to snow"],["，","",""],["我","wǒ","I"],["都","dōu","all"],["去","qù","go"],["。","",""]],"No matter whether it rains or snows, I go."],
["Since with 既然…就…","既然 + known fact，就 + suggestion","既然 (jìrán) means “since” or “now that”. The first part is a fact that both people already know. 就 (jiù) in the second part gives the suggestion or conclusion that follows from it.",[["既然","jìrán","since"],["你","nǐ","you"],["累","lèi","tired"],["了","le","(completed)"],["，","",""],["就","jiù","then"],["休息","xiūxi","to rest"],["吧","ba","(suggestion)"],["。","",""]],"Since you are tired, rest."],
["If with 要是…就…","要是 + A，就 + B","要是 (yàoshi) means “if”. It is like 如果 and is more spoken. The part with 就 (jiù) says what happens if A is true.",[["要是","yàoshi","if"],["明天","míngtiān","tomorrow"],["下雨","xià yǔ","to rain"],["，","",""],["我","wǒ","I"],["就","jiù","then"],["不","bú","not"],["去","qù","go"],["。","",""]],"If it rains tomorrow, I will not go."],
["Otherwise with 否则","A，否则 + B","否则 (fǒuzé) means “otherwise”. The first part says what must be done. 否则 introduces the bad result if it is not done.",[["快点儿","kuàidiǎnr","hurry up"],["，","",""],["否则","fǒuzé","otherwise"],["我们","wǒmen","we"],["会","huì","will"],["迟到","chídào","to be late"],["。","",""]],"Hurry up, otherwise we will be late."],
["Then, as a result with 于是","A，于是 + B","于是 (yúshì) means “so” or “then”. B happens after A and because of A. It is used to tell what happened, not to give advice.",[["我","wǒ","I"],["很","hěn","very"],["累","lèi","tired"],["，","",""],["于是","yúshì","so"],["回","huí","return"],["家","jiā","home"],["休息","xiūxi","to rest"],["了","le","(completed)"],["。","",""]],"I was tired, so I went home to rest."],
["And also with 并且","A，并且 + B","并且 (bìngqiě) joins two verb phrases and means “and also”. B adds more information to A. It is more formal than 而且.",[["他","tā","he"],["会","huì","will"],["说","shuō","say"],["中文","Zhōngwén","Chinese"],["，","",""],["并且","bìngqiě","and also"],["说","shuō","say"],["得","de","(link)"],["很","hěn","very"],["好","hǎo","good"],["。","",""]],"He can speak Chinese, and he speaks it well."],
["But with 不过","A，不过 + B","不过 (búguò) means “but” or “however”. It is softer than 但是. The second part gives a small limit to the first part.",[["这","zhè","this"],["个","ge","(measure word)"],["菜","cài","dish"],["很","hěn","very"],["好吃","hǎochī","tasty"],["，","",""],["不过","búguò","but"],["有点儿","yǒudiǎnr","a little"],["贵","guì","expensive"],["。","",""]],"This dish is tasty, but a little expensive."],
["Contrast with 而","A + 而 + B","而 (ér) shows a contrast between two things or two people. A and B are different from each other. It is used in written and formal Chinese.",[["哥哥","gēge","older brother"],["喜欢","xǐhuan","like"],["唱歌","chànggē","to sing"],["，","",""],["而","ér","and"],["我","wǒ","I"],["喜欢","xǐhuan","like"],["跳舞","tiàowǔ","to dance"],["。","",""]],"My older brother likes singing, while I like dancing."],
["Even with 甚至","A，甚至 + B","甚至 (shènzhì) means “even”. B is a stronger or more extreme example of A. It is placed at the start of B.",[["他","tā","he"],["很","hěn","very"],["忙","máng","busy"],["，","",""],["甚至","shènzhì","even"],["没有","méiyǒu","do not have"],["时间","shíjiān","time"],["吃饭","chī fàn","eat"],["。","",""]],"He is very busy. He even has no time to eat."],
["But, however with 却","Subject + 却 + verb phrase","却 (què) shows a contrast with what was said before. It comes after the subject and before the verb, never at the start of a sentence. It is often used with 但是 or 可是.",[["他","tā","he"],["很","hěn","very"],["有","yǒu","has"],["钱","qián","money"],["，","",""],["却","què","but"],["不","bú","not"],["快乐","kuàilè","happy"],["。","",""]],"He has a lot of money, but he is not happy."]
